{"_id":"HkJrnlHacking01X","name":"Hacking Rules","pages":[{"_id":"HkJrnlPg001OvrXY","name":"Overview","type":"text","text":{"content":"<h2>Hacking in MOTHERSHIP®</h2>\n<p><em>Hacking</em> can be a lucrative, if dangerous, Skill for any crewmember to acquire. Most Hacking Checks are simple: make an Intellect (Hacking) Check and if you succeed the door opens. For more robust challenges, the Warden designs a <strong>NETWORK</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Skill Requirement</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Expert Hacking</strong> — standard Intellect (Hacking) Check.</li>\n<li><strong>No Expert Hacking</strong> — roll at <strong>[-]</strong> (Disadvantage): roll 2d100 and take the worse result.</li>\n<li><strong>Sinclair 2 deck</strong> — treats Hacking as a Master Skill (bonus +20 while in inventory).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Using the Macros</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Hacking Check</strong> macro handles the full check flow: detects your character's Hacking rank, rolls at the correct mode, and on a failure prompts for the network's Response modifier before drawing from the Response Table automatically. Individual <strong>Use: [Software]</strong> macros roll the software's effect and offer to consume it from inventory.</p>\n","format":1},"sort":0,"ownership":{},"flags":{}},{"_id":"HkJrnlPg002NtwXY","name":"Networks","type":"text","text":{"content":"<h2>Networks — Example: J1C-II Research Vessel NOAH-IV</h2>\n<p>This ship has 2 networks connected via the captain's encrypted terminal. Each node shows its <em>Function | Security | Response</em>.</p>\n<pre style=\"font-family:monospace;line-height:1.5;background:#1a1a1a;color:#ccc;padding:10px;border-radius:4px;overflow-x:auto\">\n╔══════════════════[ crew.net ]══════════════════╗\n\n  INTRANET.HUB  ·  Secured Router  ·  +1 Response\n  │\n  ├──  INTRANET      ·  Unsecured Databank    ·  Null Response\n  │\n  ├──  GUEST.PC      ·  Unsecured Terminal    ·  Null Response\n  │\n  ├──  EMPLOYEE1.PC  ·  Broken Terminal       ·  Null Response\n  │\n  ├──  CAPTAIN.PDA   ·  Secured Mobile Term.  ·  +1 Response\n  │       └── Contains Captain's PEK\n  │\n  └──  CAPTAIN.PC    ·  Encrypted Terminal    ·  +3 Response\n          └── Super User ──────────────────────────────────┐\n                                                           │\n╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════╝          │\n╔══════════════════[ command.net ]════════════════╗        │\n                                                  │        │\n  FACILITIES.HUB  ·  Secured Router  ·  +2 Response  ◄────┘\n  │\n  ├──  LIFE-SUP.CTRL  ·  Hardened Infrastructure  ·  +5 Response\n  │\n  ├──  ENGINE.CTRL    ·  Hardened Infrastructure  ·  +4 Response\n  │\n  ├──  JUMPNAV.COMP   ·  Hardened Infrastructure  ·  +2 Response\n  │\n  └──  COMMS.TERM     ·  Secured Terminal          ·  +1 Response\n          └──  CORP.LINK  ·  Encrypted Uplink  ·  Null Response\n\n╚═════════════════════════════════════════════════╝\n</pre>\n<h3>Security at a Glance</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%\">\n<thead><tr><th>Node</th><th>Network</th><th>Security</th><th>Response</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>INTRANET.HUB</td><td>crew.net</td><td>Secured</td><td>+1</td><td>Central router — controls access to all crew.net nodes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>INTRANET</td><td>crew.net</td><td>Unsecured</td><td>Null</td><td>Open databank</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GUEST.PC</td><td>crew.net</td><td>Unsecured</td><td>Null</td><td>Open terminal</td></tr>\n<tr><td>EMPLOYEE1.PC</td><td>crew.net</td><td>Broken</td><td>Null</td><td>Non-functional</td></tr>\n<tr><td>CAPTAIN.PDA</td><td>crew.net</td><td>Secured</td><td>+1</td><td>Contains the Captain's PEK</td></tr>\n<tr><td>CAPTAIN.PC</td><td>crew.net</td><td>Encrypted</td><td>+3</td><td>Super User access to command.net; requires PEK</td></tr>\n<tr><td>FACILITIES.HUB</td><td>command.net</td><td>Secured</td><td>+2</td><td>Central router — entry point from CAPTAIN.PC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>LIFE-SUP.CTRL</td><td>command.net</td><td>Hardened</td><td>+5</td><td>Life support infrastructure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ENGINE.CTRL</td><td>command.net</td><td>Hardened</td><td>+4</td><td>Engine infrastructure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>JUMPNAV.COMP</td><td>command.net</td><td>Hardened</td><td>+2</td><td>Jump navigation infrastructure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>COMMS.TERM</td><td>command.net</td><td>Secured</td><td>+1</td><td>Terminal — gateway to CORP.LINK</td></tr>\n<tr><td>CORP.LINK</td><td>command.net</td><td>Encrypted</td><td>Null</td><td>External corporate uplink; requires PEK</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Key Paths for Hackers</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Quickest access to life support:</strong> Hack INTRANET.HUB (Secured) → CAPTAIN.PC (Encrypted, needs PEK from CAPTAIN.PDA) → FACILITIES.HUB (Secured) → LIFE-SUP.CTRL (Hardened [-])</li>\n<li><strong>PEK shortcut:</strong> CAPTAIN.PDA (Secured) contains the Captain's PEK — obtaining it skips the Encrypted check on CAPTAIN.PC and CORP.LINK entirely.</li>\n<li><strong>Lowest-resistance path into command.net:</strong> INTRANET.HUB → CAPTAIN.PC → FACILITIES.HUB → JUMPNAV.COMP (Hardened [-], but +2 is the lowest Response on command.net).</li>\n</ul>\n","format":1},"sort":100,"ownership":{},"flags":{}},{"_id":"HkJrnlPg003NodXY","name":"Nodes","type":"text","text":{"content":"<h2>Nodes</h2>\n<p>A node can represent a modem, hub, printer, computer, PDA, electronically controlled utility, or any other connected device. Each node is defined by its <strong>Function</strong>, <strong>Security</strong>, and <strong>Response</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Function</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Access Point</strong> — grants entry to the Network.</li>\n<li><strong>Data Storage</strong> — contains valuable information (paydata, maps, manifests, user lists, recordings, etc.).</li>\n<li><strong>Router</strong> — allows communication to pass through to other connected nodes. Controlling one could let a hacker reconfigure network paths.</li>\n<li><strong>Firewall</strong> — high Security and/or high Response node designed to hinder hacking attempts.</li>\n<li><strong>Infrastructure/Hardpoint Control</strong> — grants control of connected infrastructure (airlocks, ventilation, doors, lights, cameras, engines, reactor, life support, weapons, etc.).</li>\n<li><strong>Uplink/Relay/Bridge</strong> — connects one network to one or more separate networks.</li>\n<li><strong>Terminal</strong> — physical interface (e.g., a personal computer) that allows a user to access its contents and connect to the network.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Security Levels</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unsecured</strong> — open for anyone. No password protection or security of any kind.</li>\n<li><strong>Secured</strong> — requires a Hacking Check to gain access.</li>\n<li><strong>Hardened</strong> — heavily fortified; requires a Hacking <strong>[-]</strong> Check to defeat.</li>\n<li><strong>Encrypted</strong> — cannot be hacked. Requires the <strong>Private Encryption Key (PEK)</strong> of a user with access. PEKs can be acquired through theft, interrogation, social engineering, or hacking a lower-security node that contains the PEK.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Response</h3>\n<p>A network's <strong>Response</strong> measures how severe the reaction is from network admins, automated security, or overseer AIs. Rated from <strong>Null</strong> (no roll required) to <strong>+5</strong> (rapid and severe). Whenever a hacker fails their Hacking Check, roll on the Response Table and add the node's Response modifier.</p>\n","format":1},"sort":200,"ownership":{},"flags":{}},{"_id":"HkJrnlPg004RspXY","name":"Response Table","type":"text","text":{"content":"<h2>Response Table</h2>\n<p>Roll <strong>1d10−1</strong> and add the network node's Response modifier whenever a Hacking Check fails. Results of 9+ trigger the Tactical Response Team. The <strong>Hacking Check</strong> macro handles this automatically.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%\">\n<thead><tr><th>D10</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>00</strong></td><td>Warning message. Network RESPONSE +1.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>01</strong></td><td>Device is remotely powered off.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>02</strong></td><td>USER ACCOUNT locked out for 1d10 hours.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>03</strong></td><td>Security member dispatched to investigate. Arrives in 1d10 rounds.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>04</strong></td><td>Network creates dummy directories for the Hacker to search while a security team is sent to investigate.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>05</strong></td><td>The network and all linked networks are locked down for 1d10 hours.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>06</strong></td><td>This network and all linked networks increase security level by 1.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>07</strong></td><td>All linked networks increase RESPONSE +1.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>08</strong></td><td>Facility blackout. All non-essential electronics powered down.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>09+</strong></td><td>TACTICAL RESPONSE TEAM deployed on Seek &amp; Destroy. Arrives, heavily armed, in 1d5 rounds.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Other Response Options</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Denial of Service</strong> — NetSec floods the connection, slowing future hacking attempts.</li>\n<li><strong>Back Hack</strong> — NetSec attempts to control or destroy the terminal/deck being used.</li>\n<li><strong>Malware Attack</strong> — NetSec uses a virus to disable the hacker's hardware.</li>\n<li><strong>Power Overload</strong> — NetSec pushes excessive voltage to the hacker's hardware.</li>\n<li><strong>Identity Trace</strong> — NetSec attempts to obtain identifying information about the hacker.</li>\n<li><strong>Network Trace</strong> — NetSec attempts to locate the hacker's physical location.</li>\n</ul>\n","format":1},"sort":300,"ownership":{},"flags":{}},{"_id":"HkJrnlPg005UsrXY","name":"User Accounts","type":"text","text":{"content":"<h2>User Accounts</h2>\n<p>Instead of directly hacking a network, crewmembers can gain access to a <strong>USER ACCOUNT</strong> by obtaining a user's credentials (password, ID, badge, PEK, etc.) through theft, intimidation, or otherwise. This circumvents Hacking Checks but each account is limited in its <strong>Permissions</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Account Tiers</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Guest Account</strong> — very limited permissions: intranet (if any), company directories, online help.</li>\n<li><strong>Employee Account</strong> — permissions over anything in the employee's job description. Typically includes emails, maps, project details, and company-sensitive files.</li>\n<li><strong>Admin Account</strong> — access to everything their employees can access. Can be managers, team leaders, IT personnel, or officers on a ship.</li>\n<li><strong>Super User Account</strong> — root access to everything on the network, often on several other connected networks. If your hacker obtains root access, let them go wild — they've earned it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Tips</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>An employee badge might open all doors on the first few levels of a space station, then be used to log into a terminal connecting to the station's security network.</li>\n<li>Some networks or devices may have no user accounts at all — they can only be hacked manually.</li>\n<li>Having access to a specific User Account can provide opportunities to reach Networks unreachable through other means — a great way to engage crewmembers without the Hacking Skill.</li>\n</ul>\n","format":1},"sort":400,"ownership":{},"flags":{}},{"_id":"HkJrnlPg006EqpXY","name":"Equipment","type":"text","text":{"content":"<h2>Hacking Equipment</h2>\n<p>All items are in the <em>Hacking Equipment (1e)</em> compendium. All macros are in the <em>Hacking Macros (1e)</em> compendium.</p>\n<h3>Decks</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%\">\n<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Cost</th><th>Slots</th><th>Special</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>KOMPAN-88 'Burndeck'</td><td>450cr</td><td>1</td><td>All nodes at +1 Response</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fedorova-Turner 'Onika' Workstation</td><td>1,500cr</td><td>2</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bao-Neumann 'Superdeck'</td><td>3,500cr</td><td>5</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sato Bliss</td><td>12,000cr</td><td>3</td><td>Hardened nodes treated as Secured</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sinclair 2</td><td>50,000cr</td><td>3</td><td>Treats Hacking as Master Skill (+20 bonus while in inventory)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Wristcomms</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%\">\n<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Cost</th><th>Slots</th><th>Special</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>DVK 'Micro-80X'</td><td>25cr</td><td>—</td><td>Doors and airlocks only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Macrogram 'MX Tattler'</td><td>1,500cr</td><td>1</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>König-Seidel 'Series 6'</td><td>22,000cr</td><td>2</td><td>—</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Gear</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%\">\n<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Cost</th><th>Effect</th><th>Macro</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Brickboy</td><td>300cr</td><td>Fries hardware on a configurable triggering event.</td><td>Arm Brickboy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Faraday Bag</td><td>75cr</td><td>Blocks contents from all signals.</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Node Detector</td><td>750cr</td><td>Detects all nodes within 50m. Scout upgrade (+500cr) reveals Security and Response.</td><td>Node Detector</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Snatcher</td><td>150cr</td><td>Copies a databank while deleting the original. 1d10 rounds.</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Swiper System</td><td>200cr</td><td>Duplicates any swiped keycard. Credstick upgrade (+2,000cr) steals credits. Remote upgrade (+4,000cr) works up to 20m.</td><td>Swiper System</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Single-Use Software</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%\">\n<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Cost</th><th>Effect</th><th>Macro</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>CoyBoy</td><td>2,000cr</td><td>Reduces node Response by 1d5.</td><td>Use: CoyBoy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Icebreaker</td><td>500cr</td><td>Secured → Unsecured. IB++ (+2,000cr): Hardened → Unsecured.</td><td>Use: Icebreaker</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Keylogger</td><td>1,000cr</td><td>Records all keystrokes on a node. Install: 1d5 rounds.</td><td>Use: Keylogger</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Maze</td><td>500cr</td><td>Next NetSec Response roll is automatically ignored.</td><td>Use: Maze</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ripper2</td><td>1,000cr</td><td>Brute-force password cracker. Takes 3d10 rounds.</td><td>Use: Ripper2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Xmap</td><td>2,000cr</td><td>Retrieves a map of all nodes on this network.</td><td>Use: Xmap</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n","format":1},"sort":500,"ownership":{},"flags":{}}],"folder":null,"sort":0,"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{},"_stats":{"systemId":"mosh","systemVersion":"0.4.0","coreVersion":"11.308","createdTime":1685936572185,"modifiedTime":1693515520278,"lastModifiedBy":"6EeM38DJkcBWNAmw"}}
